  "body": "From [the spec](https://matrix.org/docs/spec/client_server/r0.5.0#id110):\r\n\r\n> Content locations are represented as Matrix Content (MXC) URIs. They look like:\r\n>\r\n> ```\r\n>mxc://<server-name>/<media-id>\r\n>\r\n><server-name> : The name of the homeserver where this content originated, e.g. matrix.org\r\n><media-id> : An opaque ID which identifies the content.\r\n>```\r\n\r\nHowever, it is not specified anywhere (as far as I can tell) what the valid character range is for these opaque `media-id`s. In particular, it doesn't specify whether the `media-id` may contain slashes - a detail that's quite semantically important for a lot of HTTP request routing implementations, which often treat a \"URL parameter\" as a string of any characters *other than* a slash, eg. as in `/media/:id` where `/media/foo/bar` would not match.",
